C/ This sample was adapted from PDF p. 53 of "Programming in HAL/S".
C/ It illustrates the use of double precision, `VECTOR` 
C/ arithmetic, and trigonometric functions.  It reads a 
C/ pair of angles for spring and another pair of angles for fall,
C/ both in degrees, and outputs single number representing the
C/ distance of an astronomical object in miles. While it doesn't 
C/ say what the angles represent, some research tells us that 
C/ they are the "apparent right ascension" and "apparent declination".
C/ As a check, I've been told that typical numbers for the star
C/ Sirius might be
C/      101.28787917, -16.71614444
C/      101.28797917, -16.71601389
C/ and that the actual distance in miles is about 50.5E12.
C/ The formula gives about 61E12, which is not too accurate, but
C/ is at least in the ballpark.  Google explains that the simple
C/ formula does not take into account "proper motion linear drift",
C/ or in other words, that Sirius (or whatever object) has itself
C/ moved between the two observations.  Curiously, the angular 
C/ values it gave me are _not_ the same as the ones in the program 
C/ GOOGLE-PARALLAX.hal, which it offered to write for me a couple 
C/ of minutes later.

  PARALLAX:
  PROGRAM;
     DECLARE EARTH_ORBIT CONSTANT(92.9E6);
     DECLARE VECTOR(2),
             SPRING_READING, FALL_READING;
     DECLARE DEVIATION SCALAR DOUBLE;
     WRITE(6) 'Angles are "apparent right ascension" and';
     WRITE(6) '"apparent declination" for some star whose';
     WRITE(6) 'distance we want to measure.';
     WRITE(6) 'Enter spring RA,DEC and fall RA,DEC :';
E                  -              -
M    READ(5) SPRING_READING, FALL_READING;
E                                          -               -
M    DEVIATION = ABVAL(3.1415926535 (SPRING_READING - FALL_READING)
                                    / 180) / 2;
     WRITE(6) 'DISTANCE=', EARTH_ORBIT / TAN(DEVIATION), 'MILES';
Y %SVCI(0);
  CLOSE PARALLAX;
  